menu "sys_session"

    config SYS_SESSION_MAX
        int "Sessions"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
          Providers attached to a peer: a slave's master, or the master's
          slaves and clients.

    #@limit @alias Calls to other boards
    config SYS_SESSION_CALLS
        int "Calls in flight"
        default 16
        range 1 64
        help
          The board's own requests to other boards waiting for an answer
          (sys_session_call); a call to this board takes none. Every call to
          another board is answered, so each write of a remote device in
          flight takes one. A slot is about 52 B.

    #@limit @alias Call timeout @unit ms
    config SYS_SESSION_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Call timeout (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 50 10000
        help
          A call without an answer this long ends with ERR_SESSION_TIMEOUT,
          unless the caller gives its own. Longer than a radio's tail, so a
          slow answer is not taken for a lost one.

    config SYS_SESSION_BOARD_ID_DEFAULT
        int "Board id until the app sets one"
        default 0
        range 0 239
        help
          The board's id when none is kept in NVS (ID). Two boards of one
          project need different ids: the app sets them with ID.

    config SYS_SESSION_PEER_LISTENERS
        int "Peer listeners"
        default 5
        range 1 8
        help
          sys_session_on_gone and sys_session_on_reached slots, each: the
          dead-man and the time (sys_system), the streams, the watches, the
          remote devices.

    config SYS_SESSION_PUSH_LISTENERS
        int "Push listeners"
        default 2
        range 1 4
        help
          sys_session_on_push slots: who takes the PUSH frames
          of a session channel.

    #@limit @alias Session timeout @unit ms
    config SYS_SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Session timeout (ms)"
        default 3000
        range 150 60000
        help
          A slave's session with nothing received for this long ends: its
          calls fail, its peers are gone, the hello starts again. The hello
          tells the peer, which sends at least every third of it. The master's
          and the clients' sessions take the dead-man's time instead
          (sys_session_set_timeout).

endmenu
